d it come to pass we talk a lot  jane about the importance of russian gas  to eu countries well actually they buy  much more oil from russia than they do  gas russia's overall crude exports last  year were expected to be this year on  current levels around 180 billion us  dollars so a very very significant sum  of money indeed the west by some 70  percent of that and the eu a big chunk  of it in all the eu relies on russia for  around 25  of its crude imports so this is why it  is such a big deal it would deprive the  kremlin of a lot of money the other  reason i think that the eu is staggering  this increa this measure and not  bringing it in straight away is because  they don't want to push up the oil price  suddenly which would inadvertently  potentially make even more money for  vladimir putin so that's why i think  there is going to be this phased  approach it's part of a number of other  measures that have been introduced by  the eu today they've also announced that  spur bank which is russia's biggest bank  is being kicked off the swift system  which is a communications messaging  system that helps facilitate  cross-border money transfers around the  world and it's also banning three  russian broadcasters from the eu's  airways they've also incidentally as  part of this package named several  individuals who they think are  responsible for the massacre in bucco  recently and uh that is something that  angela van der lion stated in her  address to the european parliament  earlier this morning but as i say it is  this measure on oil that is the real  showstopper a lot of people will be  skeptical as to whether this will work  they will say well hang on won't russia  just sell more crude to other countries  at a knock down price and indeed we do  know that they've been doing that with  recent oil embargoes from the uk and the  united states there has been some  evidence of russia trying to sell its  oil cheaply to china and india for  example but they have a problem in doing  that for a start as i said earlier on  most of their crude exports currently go  to the west so there will be a challenge  in getting it across to buyers in the  east there is one pipeline that runs  from russia to china for oil but so that  would effectively mean that those uh  exports would have to be done by by  seabourn methods in other words by big  oil tankers and there's a question there  whether the carriers the owners the  shipping companies the tanker owners  would necessarily want to do that they'd  be risking for example being punished  for sanctions busting by some counts  they also would find their insurance  premiums very very high because a lot of  those crude exports would have to go via  black sea ports and we've seen for  example how effective the ukrainians  have been to attacking the russian navy  so there'd be challenges there as well  but overall it's a big big measure  ian thanks very much
 

